# Reflection

This project was a change from the previous projects because I focused mainly on understanding architecture rather than implementing individual hardware modules.

Previously, I built components such as UART, SPI, ROM, RAM, a Register File and an ALU separately.

This project showed how these types of components can become parts of a larger system.

One of the most important concepts I learned was that the CPU does not need to communicate directly with every peripheral. Instead, a bus provides a common communication system between the CPU, memory and peripherals.

I also learned about memory-mapped I/O, where peripherals can be assigned addresses and accessed using memory operations.

This concept will become particularly important when I start learning about RISC-V and Linux.

---

# Problems

The biggest challenge was understanding the difference between the individual hardware components I had already built and the architecture connecting them together.

It was easy to understand what a UART or RAM does individually, but understanding how the CPU communicates with them through a bus required looking at the system as a whole.

Creating block diagrams helped me understand the relationships between the components.

---

# What I Would Improve

I would like to eventually implement a small version of this architecture in Verilog and run it on an FPGA.

For now however, the purpose of this project is to understand the architecture before implementing it.

